# A place of the j-line is v_∞ iff j has a pole

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.eq_jLinePlaceInfty_iff_ord_jGen_neg`
(`413ef15d-0bcf-59bf-a139-83d5e14af46e`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `1de9d5da-fc0b-5eb9-8ff9-37e9c945f759`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Here $jq$ denotes the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ (the
$q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$, with $\mathrm{jNumQ}$ the rational-coefficient power
series obtained from the integral numerator series), and $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ is the simple
extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $jq$ inside the field of Laurent series;
[`ModularCurve.jGen`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L268) is $jq$ viewed as an element of that field. A
place of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is, by the project's definition, a
valuation subring of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ which contains the image of $\mathbb{Q}$, is not
the whole field, and is a principal ideal ring; for such a place $v$ and $f$ in the field,
$v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$ is minus the logarithm of the value of $f$ under the adic valuation attached to
$v$. The place [`ModularCurve.jLinePlaceInfty`](def/ModularCurve_JLinePlaces.html#L58) is the
transport, along the isomorphism $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,\mathbb{Q}\cong\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$
sending the variable to $jq$ (available since $jq$ is transcendental over $\mathbb{Q}$), of the
place at infinity of $\mathbb{Q}(T)$, i.e. of the valuation subring of the degree valuation. The
assertion is that for every place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ one has $v
=$ `jLinePlaceInfty` if and only if $v.\mathrm{ord}\,(jGen) < 0$.

This identifies the place at infinity of the $j$-line $X(1)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ as the unique place
at which $j$ has a pole, all other places lying in the subring where $j$ is integral. It is used by
[`ModularCurve.restrict_eq_jLinePlaceInfty_iff`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.restrict_eq_jLinePlaceInfty_iff)
in the analysis of places of modular function fields above the $j$-line.

